! Verse Mesh — Design Narrative

!! What a Peer Owns

A lararium peer owns ''bags'' (Automerge documents), not wikis.
A wiki is a ''recipe'' — an ordered composition of bags.
The peer assembles a wiki on demand by stacking bags in priority order.
The same CRDT source bags yield different rendered views for different operators
and different application surfaces.

The invariant: bags persist, wikis compose.

!! N Local Clocks — Polychronous CRDT Mesh

The Verse mesh runs ''N local clocks'', one per TW5 VM instance.
Each VM constitutes one clock domain (Signal/INRIA polychronous model, Berry 1991).
Rendezvous between clock domains happens ''only at CRDT merge boundaries'' —
when Automerge delivers a remote change, the island's wiki shore queues it for a
landing paced by host task.

No global conductor exists.
No peer commands another peer's tick rate.
Each island owns its own pacing, inside its own worker.
The CRDT carries state across the water in causal order.
The wiki shore lands it in TW5 slice by slice.

!! VM Pool — Every Engine in a Worker

''No TW5 engine runs on a main thread, on any platform.'' The vessel's main thread coordinates a
pool and holds no engine: it keeps `DocHandle` references, the transferred sync ports and the
painted surface. Each island boots its own Repo over a transferred port and derives its tiddlers
from its own CRDT doc (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/island-protocol.ts`, the Island Sovereignty Law).

```
Vessel main thread (coordinates; holds no engine)
 ├── island pool            ← VesselIslandPoolCore: wela / anu residency + pin, LRU
 │    ├── island[wiki A]    ← a Worker (worker_threads on node, Web Worker in a browser)
 │    │    ├── Repo-in-island        ← CRDT sync over the transferred syncPort
 │    │    ├── TW5Engine             ← one TW5 wiki — the shared world graph
 │    │    ├── nalu engine           ← applies ONE change, synchronously
 │    │    ├── IslandAdaptor         ← the wiki shore: one landing queue across all bags; outbound writes
 │    │    └── Camera[story-river]
 │    │         ├── widget tree      ← wiki.makeTranscludeWidget("$:/core/ui/RootTemplate", ...)
 │    │         └── VM-side document ← $tw.fakeDocument; frames cross to the main thread
 │    └── island[wiki B] …
 │
 └── painted surfaces (browser) ← each camera's frames applied as real HTML in a shadow root;
                                   DOM events and field values return to the island
```

Residency runs ''two-state'': an island stands `wela` (a live worker) or `anu` (no worker), with an
orthogonal pin that exempts an island from cooling. No warm middle tier stands
(`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/residency-tiers#/third-tier-gate`); heating an island spawns a fresh
worker, which declares `ea` when it breathes.

A node vessel paints nothing. Its islands project to disk in-island, and a headless render reads
`$tw.fakeDocument`.

!! Camera Model — Story River Is the First Camera

A ''camera'' = one view frustum over the TW5 wiki world-state.

!!! Three-Tree Chain — Per Camera

Each camera runs its own three-tree chain, the same chain TW5 already runs for
the Story River:

```
wikitext source (tiddler body)
    ↓  wiki.parseTiddler() / wiki.parseText()
parse tree  (ParseTreeNode[])
    ↓  widget.makeChildWidgets()
widget tree (Widget graph)
    ↓  widget.render(container, null)
VM-side document (TW5FakeElement tree)
    ↓  serialize → frame → paint
rendered output
```

`wiki.makeTranscludeWidget(rootTiddler, { document, parentWidget })` creates the
widget tree and binds it to a `document`.
Each camera receives its own `document` instance — the render surfaces stay separate.

`mountCamera(engine, { rootTiddler, document, container })` (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-camera.ts`)
wires the parse→widget→document chain for one camera, registers the camera's `change` listener, and
returns a teardown.

!!! Camera Surface Contract

Each camera holds:
* its own ''widget tree'' — TW5 widget graph rooted at a filter or transclusion tiddler
* its own ''VM-side document'' — the render target, living in the island beside the engine
* its own ''frame grain'', declared at mount — the unit of absolute state its frames key by (`camera-mount` C-6)
* its own input/output surface — user events in, wiki writes out

A browser camera's widget tree never binds `window.document`. The camera renders into its VM-side
document; the main thread paints that camera's frames and returns events and sensed state
(`camera-mount` C-7). Every frame carries absolute state for its key with a monotone revision, and no
frame carries a delta (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/dreamdom#/the-ruling`).

!!! Shared World Graph — Inverted Control

All cameras in one island ''share one TW5 wiki'' (world graph).
The wiki fires one `change` event for the titles its `nextTick` queue gathered.
Each widget tree registered via `wiki.addEventListener("change", tree.refresh)`
reacts independently — widget trees with no dependency on the changed tiddlers
return `refresh()` in O(1) without repaint.

The ''view frustum'' lives in the widget tree's root filter tiddler, not in the landing queue.
The landing queue carries no camera identity — ''inverted control''.

!!! Input Path — No New Machinery

Cameras that accept user input (typing in the Story River, drawing on a canvas camera)
dispatch events through the widget tree's standard TW5 event bus.
A `dispatchEvent({ type: "tm-verse-event", uri, listenable })` call travels up the
widget tree to the root widget, where `reaction-router.ts` catches it.
Outbound writes — when a user saves a tiddler from any camera — go through
`IslandAdaptor.saveTiddler → store.writeFamily()`, the same path the Story River uses.
No new write-back machinery is necessary for additional cameras.

A browser camera's input starts on the painted surface. The main thread relays a DOM event by the
render-id the island stamped on the node (`wiki:dom-event`), and a text field's whole current value
on a sibling message (`wiki:dom-input`); the island invokes the handler TW5 itself bound, and from
there the widget tree runs as if the event had fired locally.

The TW5 Story River is the ''first camera'' — the primary rendered view
into the Verse graph for browser peers.
It does not occupy the only camera slot.

The ''second camera slot'' targets a ''canvas camera'': a DOM-hosting surface that never rasterises
and takes no vendor's name (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/wikispace-canvasspace-ontology#/the-surface-law`).
It renders a different projection of the same bag state —
spatial, relational, or visual-graph views of the same Automerge documents. Verse-style cameras
stand beside other camera mounts; one protocol carries them, and the grain varies per camera.

Further camera slots remain open for:
* Mobile native views (React Native / Capacitor)
* Background portal-spaces, comms links, nexus leyline feeds, remote signal feeds
* Household / community / civil / interplanetary lararium implementations
* Headless render targets (PDF export, MCP tool surface, LLM context projection)
* Spectator/witness views with different read authority

''Causal islands, fractal:'' causal islands exist inside the lararium (bag boundaries),
outside the lararium (peer-to-peer CRDT sync), inside the TW5 VM (per-camera tick
domains), and outside the TW5 VM (each island in its own worker, on its own clock).
As above, so below.

!!! Multi-Camera Example

```
Island (one worker, one TW5 wiki)
├── Story River camera
│   ├── document: VM-side; frames painted main-side into a shadow root
│   ├── root: $:/core/ui/RootTemplate
│   ├── frame grain: the whole surface (stands); tiddler frame + the river's order (ruled, unbuilt)
│   └── wiki.addEventListener("change", storyWidget.refresh)
│
├── Canvas camera                                   (unbuilt)
│   ├── document: its own VM-side DreamDOM document — hosts DOM, never rasterises
│   ├── root filter: [tag[lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/kumu-device]]
│   ├── frame grain: the shape
│   └── wiki.addEventListener("change", canvasWidget.refresh)
│
└── Mini-map camera                                 (unbuilt)
    ├── document: its own VM-side document
    ├── root filter: [all[tiddlers]tag[lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/spatial]]
    ├── cadence: a slower emit than the river's — background priority
    └── wiki.addEventListener("change", minimapWidget.refresh)
```

Cameras that accept user input (typing, drawing) register outbound handlers
(`saveTiddler`, `dispatchEvent`) on their widget tree.
Input from any camera writes back through `IslandAdaptor.saveTiddler` →
`store.put()` — the same outbound path the Story River uses.

!! Visibility Gate — The Scale Boundary

The wiki shore lands the changes of every queued bag, in slices of at most 200.

A visibility gate would defer part of that work:
land only the tiddlers the ''active story river currently renders'', and hold
background bags (cold layers not in the active recipe view) until the next explicit
navigation event.

This gate stands unbuilt.
It names the correct scale boundary before the project reaches it.

!! Wiring Law — What Stands, and What Stays Unbuilt

''What stands.'' One queue per wiki, at the wiki shore: `IslandAdaptor` holds one landing queue
across every CRDT bag and lands it in slices, and the nalu engine
(`packages/lararium-tw5/src/modules/nalu-engine.ts`) applies each change synchronously. The wiki
fires `change` on its own `nextTick` schedule, apart from the shore's slices;
every mounted widget tree sees it, and only trees depending on the changed tiddlers repaint.

```typescript
// In the island, beside the engine (packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-projection.ts):
const stop = mountCamera(engine, {
  rootTiddler: TW5_ROOT_TEMPLATE,
  document:    $tw.fakeDocument,     // VM-side; never window.document
  container:   storyContainer,
});
// Each wiki `change` marks a coalesce gate; the gate posts the newest whole-surface
// snapshot as one `projection:frame` event, carrying html, css and a monotone rev.
```

The browser island mounts one story camera and one stylesheet reader this way, and the main thread
applies each frame to a shadow root. The coalesce window stays pinned near one display frame:
newest wins, and a burst of changes collapses to one post.

''What stays unbuilt.''
* A second camera on one island, and with it a document per camera beyond `$tw.fakeDocument`.
* Per-camera tick rates and drain budgets. No `startRenderLoop` and no per-camera accumulator
  stand in code; one coalesce gate paces the frames of the whole surface.
* A frame grain finer than the whole surface. The ruling names where the grain goes and orders no
  build: whole-surface frames stand until a measurement says a camera needs finer
  (`dreamdom#/the-ruling`).
* The DreamDOM document itself, which grows out of the standing projection, never beside it.

!! Tick Sources by Platform

Every row names a pace ''inside a worker''; no row places an engine on a main thread.

| Platform | Where the engine runs | Landing pace (wiki shore) | Paint |
|---|---|---|---|
| Browser | Web Worker | a host task between slices: `scheduler.yield` where the worker offers one, else a `MessageChannel` message | main thread applies `projection:frame` to a shadow root |
| Node | `worker_threads` Worker | a host task between slices: `setImmediate` | none — disk projection in-island; `$tw.fakeDocument` for a headless render |
| Mobile, civil, interplanetary | a worker of that runtime (unbuilt) | that runtime's host task | that platform's surface |

The landing queue stays identical across all platforms.
The host-task seat changes; the landing contract does not. The nalu engine holds no timer.

